[8/12, 10:33 PM] .: SCD stands for Slowly Changing Dimension. It is mainly a data warehousing concept, not a special SQL command.
[8/12, 10:33 PM] .: It deals with a simple question:

What should we do when information about a customer/product changes?
[8/12, 10:34 PM] .: There are 6 commonly discussed types,
[8/12, 10:38 PM] Gaurav: Type 0 — Keep original
You never change the original value.
[8/12, 10:38 PM] Gaurav: Type 1 — Overwrite
You simply replace the old value.
[8/12, 10:39 PM] Gaurav: Type 2 — Keep full history ⭐
This is the most important SCD type for interviews and data warehousing.

Instead of updating the old row, you create a new row.
[8/12, 10:41 PM] Gaurav: Type 3 — Keep limited history
Instead of creating a new row, you keep the old value and current value in separate columns.
[8/12, 10:41 PM] Gaurav: Type 4 — Separate history table
You keep the current data in one table and historical data in another.
[8/12, 10:42 PM] Gaurav: Type 6 — Hybrid approach
Type 6 combines ideas from Type 1 + Type 2 + Type 3.

It can maintain:

Current value
Previous value
Historical rows
